begin{abstract}
Feature attributions are usually computed once, by a randomised, budget-limited estimator, and read as if they were the attribution.
We ask how far one SHAP run is a reliable basis for the conclusion a reader draws from it, the top feature, the top-$k$ set or a sign, rather than for its average error.
We fix the value function, take its exact Shapley value as reference, and measure per instance how often a run returns the exact top-3 set, with and without tolerance for near ties, for four model classes on five tabular datasets.
At the \texttt{shap} default budget, on the two datasets large enough to be sampled (one trained model each; $40$ and $60$ instances), a run returns the exact top-3 set with probability at least $0.9$ for $0.90$--$1.00$ of the instances of every cell, and for all of them once swaps across near ties are forgiven; the exceptions are small-margin instances, which mean correlation with the exact values would not flag.
With three to four times as many coalitions as features the tolerance-aware share is $0.65$--$0.97$ for the non-linear models, and it is near zero at twice as many.
With a $50$-row background, two exactly explained backgrounds agree on the top-3 set for $0.27$--$0.69$ of the instances, against $0.91$--$0.99$ for two explainer runs, so at this background size the run is not the weakest link.
A certificate computed from one run is not a clear improvement over running twice.
The three smaller datasets, where the default enumerates all coalitions, inform only the smaller budgets and the other noise sources.
\end{abstract}

